# Scanwick

**AI-powered website auditor.** Paste a URL and get performance metrics, SEO checks, screenshots at three screen sizes, prioritized fixes with code snippets, a client-ready summary and a PDF report.

![Scanwick results](docs/results.png)

## Features

- **Performance audit** (mobile or desktop) with the Google PageSpeed Insights API: score, LCP, CLS, page weight, number of requests and the 5 problems that would save the most loading time.
- **SEO checks:** title and meta description length, number of H1 headings, image alt text, mobile viewport tag and page language.
- **Responsive preview:** real browser screenshots at 375, 768 and 1440 px (Playwright), with horizontal-overflow detection.
- **AI assistant (Groq):**
  - prioritized fix recommendations with steps and a code snippet adapted to your stack (React, Next.js, WordPress, HTML/CSS);
  - a short, jargon-free client summary in English, French or Turkish.
- **PDF report** with everything above, ready to send to a client.
- **Audit history** saved in MongoDB. Open a past audit without calling Google again.

![AI recommendations](docs/ai.png)

## How the AI stays honest

A language model can invent problems, so Scanwick does not trust its text:

1. The model never sees the page itself. It receives a short list of **facts** (for example `lcp: 6.6 s`, `seo.description: warn: 190 characters`).
2. Each recommendation must cite the **keys** of the facts it is based on.
3. The server checks every key against the real audit data, drops any recommendation without valid evidence, and displays **its own values** instead of the model's wording.
4. Text coming from the audited website is treated as data, not as instructions.

## Tech stack

| Part                       | Tools                                         |
| -------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |
| Front end                  | React, TypeScript, Vite, Tailwind CSS         |
| Back end                   | Node.js, Express, TypeScript (run with `tsx`) |
| Data                       | MongoDB (Mongoose)                            |
| Browser automation and PDF | Playwright (Chromium)                         |
| Performance data           | Google PageSpeed Insights API                 |
| AI                         | Groq API (`openai/gpt-oss-20b` by default)    |

## Getting started

### Requirements

- Node.js 20 or newer
- A free [Google PageSpeed Insights API key](https://console.cloud.google.com/) (enable "PageSpeed Insights API")
- A free [MongoDB Atlas](https://www.mongodb.com/atlas) cluster (optional: without it, audits are simply not saved)
- A free [Groq API key](https://console.groq.com/) (optional: only needed for the AI assistant)

### Run it locally

```bash
git clone https://github.com/oussamaelouragini/scanwick.git
cd scanwick
```

**Server** (terminal 1):

```bash
cd server
npm install
npx playwright install chromium
cp .env.example .env     # on Windows PowerShell: Copy-Item .env.example .env
# edit .env and add your keys
npm run dev
```

**Client** (terminal 2):

```bash
cd client
npm install
npm run dev
```

Open http://localhost:5173.

### Environment variables (`server/.env`)

| Variable       | Required | Description                                                |
| -------------- | -------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |
| `PSI_API_KEY`  | yes      | Google PageSpeed Insights API key                          |
| `MONGODB_URI`  | no       | MongoDB connection string. Without it, history is disabled |
| `GROQ_API_KEY` | no       | Groq API key. Without it, the AI buttons return an error   |
| `GROQ_MODEL`   | no       | Override the default Groq model                            |

Never commit your `.env` file.

## API

| Route                          | Description                                             |
| ------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------- |
| `POST /api/audit`              | Runs a PageSpeed audit (`url`, `strategy`) and saves it |
| `POST /api/inspect`            | Opens the page in Chromium: screenshots and SEO checks  |
| `GET /api/audits`              | The 10 most recent saved audits                         |
| `POST /api/ai/recommendations` | Fix recommendations with verified evidence              |
| `POST /api/ai/summary`         | Client summary (`en`, `fr` or `tr`)                     |
| `POST /api/report`             | Builds the PDF report                                   |

## Project structure

```
scanwick/
├── client/              React app
│   └── src/
│       ├── components/  ScoreCard, ProblemsList, SeoChecklist, ResponsivePreview, AiPanel, ...
│       ├── UrlForm.tsx
│       ├── rating.ts    Google's thresholds for the score, LCP and CLS
│       └── types.ts
└── server/              Express API
    └── src/
        ├── index.ts     routes
        ├── inspect.ts   screenshots and SEO data (Playwright)
        ├── seo.ts       SEO checks
        ├── ai.ts        Groq calls and evidence validation
        ├── report.ts    HTML report and PDF
        ├── db.ts
        └── models/Audit.ts
```

## Limitations

- PageSpeed results vary a little between runs of the same URL. This is normal for Lighthouse.
- Screenshots are not saved in the database (they are large), so they are captured again when you open a past audit.
- There is no user login yet. Do not expose this server publicly without adding authentication and rate limits, because it uses your API keys.
- Groq model names change over time. If the default model is retired, set `GROQ_MODEL`.

